1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:07,956
Hello there, Robbie.

2
00:00:07,956 --> 00:00:29,802
What are you doing up this late?

3
00:00:29,802 --> 00:00:30,302
Reading.

4
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:30,502
Reading?

5
00:00:30,302 --> 00:00:31,280
At this hour?

6
00:00:31,280 --> 00:01:25,383
Ah, come on, Robbie.

7
00:01:25,383 --> 00:01:30,168
What are you doing up this late?

8
00:01:30,168 --> 00:01:31,274
Things on my mind.

9
00:01:31,274 --> 00:01:38,673
Do you care to talk about them?

10
00:01:38,673 --> 00:01:39,073
Sure,

11
00:01:39,073 --> 00:01:41,918
if you don't mind listening.

12
00:01:41,918 --> 00:01:47,207
Robbie, Robbie, remember me? Robbie Robbie

13
00:01:47,207 --> 00:01:48,257
I'm your mother.

14
00:01:48,257 --> 00:01:51,565
If you have something you want to talk about,

15
00:01:51,565 --> 00:01:53,163
I'm always prepared to listen.

16
00:01:53,163 --> 00:01:56,140
You haven't been around much lately.

17
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:59,118
So that's it.

18
00:01:59,118 --> 00:02:02,096
OK, let's talk.

19
00:02:02,096 --> 00:02:05,074
You and Dad are like ships that pass in the night.

20
00:02:05,074 --> 00:02:08,052
Dad works hard, and he works late.

21
00:02:08,052 --> 00:02:10,448
You work hard on all your committees,

22
00:02:10,448 --> 00:02:12,230
and you work late.

23
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:14,008
I thought you were proud of the work I do.

24
00:02:14,008 --> 00:02:15,616
I am, Mom.

25
00:02:15,616 --> 00:02:16,986
Real proud.

26
00:02:16,986 --> 00:02:19,353
You are one fantastic mom,

27
00:02:19,353 --> 00:02:24,151
but ... but I've been noticing

28
00:02:24,151 --> 00:02:25,920
how little quality time

29
00:02:25,920 --> 00:02:28,898
you spend with Dad and me ... and the family.

30
00:02:28,898 --> 00:02:35,758
It's a real problem, Robbie.

31
00:02:35,758 --> 00:02:38,470
I know it.

32
00:02:38,470 --> 00:02:41,810
I'm concerned.

33
00:02:41,810 --> 00:02:44,788
There must be a way

34
00:02:44,788 --> 00:02:49,744
that Dad and you can spend more time together.

35
00:02:49,744 --> 00:02:51,366
Well, we always talk about

36
00:02:51,366 --> 00:02:54,222
taking a vacation together--with the family.

37
00:02:54,222 --> 00:02:56,372
I think you ought to

38
00:02:56,372 --> 00:02:59,467
take a vacation away from the family--alone.

39
00:02:59,467 --> 00:03:01,656
Kind of a second honeymoon.

40
00:03:01,656 --> 00:03:03,359
It would be wonderful,

41
00:03:03,359 --> 00:03:06,634
but our schedules won't allow it.

42
00:03:06,634 --> 00:03:08,505
I think I have an idea.

43
00:03:08,505 --> 00:03:10,589
You do?

44
00:03:10,589 --> 00:03:10,922
Yup.

45
00:03:10,922 --> 00:03:12,922
I think I have an idea

46
00:03:12,922 --> 00:03:14,876
that will bring Dad and you

47
00:03:14,876 --> 00:03:17,045
together in a more scheduled way.

48
00:03:17,045 --> 00:03:19,523
What is it?

49
00:03:19,523 --> 00:03:19,737
Well,

50
00:03:19,737 --> 00:03:21,452
you know how Dad is always talking about

51
00:03:21,452 --> 00:03:22,501
the kids in the ward

52
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:26,094
and how important it is for them to be paid attention to?

53
00:03:26,094 --> 00:03:26,709
Yes.

54
00:03:26,709 --> 00:03:27,324
Well ...

55
00:03:27,324 --> 00:03:28,457
and how hard it is

56
00:03:28,457 --> 00:03:32,003
because the doctors and nurses are so busy?

57
00:03:32,003 --> 00:03:32,571
Yes.

58
00:03:32,571 --> 00:03:36,485
Well, how would it be if you took some time

59
00:03:36,485 --> 00:03:39,891
to work with Dad towards solving that problem?

60
00:03:39,891 --> 00:03:41,382
I don't get it.

61
00:03:41,382 --> 00:03:46,963
Like setting up a regular weekly reading program.

62
00:03:46,963 --> 00:03:47,163
You and Dad.

63
00:03:46,963 --> 00:03:48,503
You and Dr. Philip Stewart--

64
00:03:48,503 --> 00:03:51,485
going to the children's ward once or twice a week

65
00:03:51,485 --> 00:03:53,841
and reading to them.

66
00:03:53,841 --> 00:03:55,259
Not bad.

67
00:03:55,259 --> 00:03:58,237
Not a bad idea, Robbie.

68
00:03:58,237 --> 00:03:59,308
As a matter of fact,

69
00:03:59,308 --> 00:04:01,786
it fits right in with something I'm working on right now

70
00:04:01,786 --> 00:04:03,215
with the school-board committee.

71
00:04:03,215 --> 00:04:04,193
What's that?

72
00:04:04,193 --> 00:04:05,318
I've been trying

73
00:04:05,318 --> 00:04:07,171
to work out a program

74
00:04:07,171 --> 00:04:09,765
in the public school

75
00:04:09,765 --> 00:04:13,127
that will bring parents and teachers together once a week

76
00:04:13,127 --> 00:04:16,105
to read to the students--their own children, really.

77
00:04:16,105 --> 00:04:19,083
By doing that, it will encourage reading.

78
00:04:19,083 --> 00:04:21,992
So it might fit in with a program

79
00:04:21,992 --> 00:04:24,060
for reading to the kids in the hospital.

80
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:25,038
You're right.

81
00:04:25,038 --> 00:04:26,838
We'll do it!

82
00:04:26,838 --> 00:04:29,816
I'm going to talk to Daddy about it right now.

83
00:04:29,816 --> 00:04:33,094
But Dad was so exhausted when he came home from work.

84
00:04:33,094 --> 00:04:34,972
Why don't you talk to him about it tomorrow?

85
00:04:34,972 --> 00:04:39,761
You are a very smart young man, Robbie.

86
00:04:39,761 --> 00:04:41,928
I think I'll wait until tomorrow.

87
00:04:41,928 --> 00:04:43,986
You won't forget, will you?

88
00:04:43,986 --> 00:04:45,884
Believe me, I won't.

89
00:04:45,884 --> 00:04:47,248
It is a great idea,

90
00:04:47,248 --> 00:04:49,862
and I promise you I won't forget.

91
00:04:49,862 --> 00:04:51,840
Thanks, Mom.

92
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:54,840
Thank you, Robbie.
